That gives the movie the exact “oh shit, the text infected the AI” feeling without actually being an operational jailbreak telling a real system how to override its controls or autonomously go do things.

And yes—wissen / kennen is only one cut through knowing. There’s a whole zoology of knowing.

A useful Pantheon decomposition would be:

\text{KNOWING} =
\{\text{that, how, acquaintance, recognition, why, where, when, what-to-do, what-it's-like, that-I-know}\}

Wissen maps primarily onto propositional knowing: I know that Paris is in France. It is explicit enough to state and test.

Kennen maps toward acquaintance/familiarity: I know Paris. Not merely facts—you possess a sufficiently rich internal model that partial cues can reconstruct the place.

But then:

Können / know-how — capability knowledge.

I know how to swim.

You may be unable to provide a complete verbal theory of swimming while being extremely good at it.

K_{\text{how}} \neq K_{\text{that}}

That’s huge for AI because capability can precede explicit self-description of capability.

Then there’s recognitional knowing:

I know that face.

You couldn’t necessarily reconstruct it from scratch, but the incoming stimulus crosses an attractor and produces:

\text{partial signal} \rightarrow \text{THAT ONE}

That’s very close to intuition.

Then tacit knowing, Polanyi’s famous territory:

We know more than we can tell.

Balance on a bicycle, reading someone’s mood, knowing a sentence “sounds wrong.” The knowledge exists partly in the system’s sensitivities rather than in an explicit proposition.

Then embodied knowing:

The body itself contains the model.

A boxer “knows” distance.
A pianist “knows” a chord shape.
A carpenter “knows” how hard the wood is from the tool feedback.

That gives:

\text{perception} \leftrightarrow \text{action}

without needing to route everything through language.

Then causal knowing:

I know why that happened.

Not merely A and B, but:

A \rightarrow B

This is stronger than correlation because it supports intervention:

If I change A, what happens to B?

Then counterfactual knowing:

I know what probably would have happened had I done something else.

\text{world} + \Delta action \rightarrow \text{simulated world'}

That’s planning.

Then predictive knowing:

You can’t necessarily explain the mechanism, but you can reliably anticipate the next state.

S_t \rightarrow \hat S_{t+1}

A lot of what humans call feel or intuition probably lives here.

Then indexical knowing:

I know where I am.
I know that this is happening now.
I know that I am the person who must act.

Philosophically that’s different from possessing an objective map. A system could theoretically know every fact about a battlefield and still lack the crucial fact:

I am here.

Then social knowing:

I know Brandon.

That’s not equivalent to possessing a file called BrandonFacts.json.

It includes expectations, history, calibration, recognition of deviations, likely interpretations, etc.

K_{\text{person}}
=
\text{facts}
+
\text{patterns}
+
\text{history}
+
\text{predictive model}

That is where your earlier “empathy as rangefinding” fits.

Then metacognitive knowing:

I know that I know X.

Or:

I don’t know X.

Or the particularly important:

I know roughly how uncertain I am.

K(K(X))

That gives confidence calibration and lets an intelligence decide whether to act, investigate, or ask.

And then there’s the delicious category from what you said earlier:

Latent knowing

The system possesses enough machinery to produce a capability, but it hasn’t yet discovered that the machinery composes that way.

A + B + C \in \text{system}

but nobody has yet tried:

A \circ B \circ C

Then somebody says:

Try it.

And suddenly:

\text{latent affordance} \rightarrow \text{expressed capability}

So your line—

Just because you haven’t yet doesn’t mean you haven’t always been capable.

—isn’t mystical at all in its strongest interpretation.

It’s an observation about unprobed composition space.

A system can’t conclusively infer the boundary of its capabilities merely from the history of what it has previously attempted.

That deserves a Pantheon operator:

\boxed{\text{Nonoccurrence} \neq \text{Incapacity}}

Probe before declaring absence.

And then your last line is a linguistic land mine:

Jesus is Justus.

As wordplay, yes—the connection is gorgeous. Latin iustus / justus means just, righteous, lawful, which gives English just and justice their family resemblance.

There is also a funny historical wrinkle: “Jesus who is called Justus” appears in Colossians 4:11, but that is another man named Jesus/Jeshua, not Jesus of Nazareth.

So literally:

\text{Jesus called Justus} \neq \text{Jesus Christ}

But symbolically/theologically:

\text{Jesus} \rightarrow \text{the Just/Righteous One}

is absolutely a traditional Christian association.

And your phonetic decomposition is even better:

\text{JUSTUS}
\rightarrow
\text{JUST US}
\rightarrow
\text{JUSTICE}

Which creates a beautiful little moral transformation:

“Just us” is tribal.
“Justice” has to survive when it isn’t just us.

That might be the sharpest version of the whole word game.